air/OS (Haiku arm64): OpenGL and OpenGL ES for the Cubie A7S from Mesa 26.2.4,
Mesa's EGL vendor library for libglvnd (libEGL_mesa.so.0) with the Gallium
drivers zink, on the PowerVR Vulkan driver, and softpipe. Applied after
mesa-haiku-pvr.patch; it touches none of that patch's files.

Haiku's EGL and hgl code is the Raspberry Pi 4 / ROCK 5 port (Mesa 25.3.6:
tools/rpi4/mesa, tools/rock5-itx/mesa/mesa-haiku-native.patch), whose
upstream base is unchanged in 26.2.4, without its V3D and Mali screens and
shared V3D bitmaps, plus the zink pieces of the air/OS x86_64 fork
(airos-25.3.6-x86_64: 7fc91dc, 2c7a86b, 8f699d2, 33656fb).

  src/egl/drivers/haiku/egl_haiku.cpp
      - zink when the "powervr" kernel driver published its device
        (HAIKU_PVR_DEVICE or /dev/graphics/powervr/0) and neither
        LIBGL_ALWAYS_SOFTWARE nor another GALLIUM_DRIVER says otherwise;
        softpipe when zink is not wanted or no screen comes up (no
        libvulkan.so.1, no Vulkan device). PVR_I_WANT_A_BROKEN_VULKAN_DRIVER
        is set for the zink screen's creation only, unless already set
      - GPU screens present by reading the frame back into a bitmap that the
        window's BitmapHook shows, two bitmaps, the displayed one only
        written after SetBitmap() gave it back (the Pi's window presentation)
      - contexts with their attributes (GL core/compat and ES versions,
        flags, robustness, priority, no-error, release behaviour), client
        APIs and configs from what the screen supports (RGBA8888, depth 24,
        stencil 8, pbuffers up to the largest texture), window size
        tracking and eglQuerySurface, pbuffers that do not swap,
        eglMakeCurrent that restores the old binding on failure, display
        reference counting
      - haiku_mesa_readback_band_bytes() (Summit's optional readback hint):
        0, whole rectangles, for zink; -1 otherwise
      - printf()s become TRACE()
  src/egl/meson.build, egl*.sym, egl*-symbols.txt
      link zink and xmlconfig; export the readback hint
  src/gallium/frontends/hgl/hgl.c, hgl_context.h
      present and size hooks for the EGL driver; display targets only for
      software screens; a depth/stencil format the screen has; contexts
      from st_context_attribs; drawables destroyed with the framebuffer
  src/gallium/frontends/hgl/meson.build, winsys/sw/hgl/meson.build
      depend on the generated format header (a parallel build race)
  src/gallium/auxiliary/target-helpers/inline_sw_helper.h
      include zink_public.h (sw_screen_create_zink() did not compile)
  src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_bo.c
      a screen that failed before zink_bo_init() tore down an uninitialized
      cache mutex and asserted: the softpipe fallback crashed
  src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_screen.c
      disk cache key: without a build-id note, the library file's timestamp
      (else no cache), so a new zink never reads an old one's shaders
  src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_context.c
      zink_wait_on_batch() on a lost device: the flush submits nothing, so
      the batch has no id; report the loss and return instead of asserting
      (debugoptimized builds aborted the GL program after a GPU reset)
  src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_screen.c, zink_resource.c
      host-cached memory that is not coherent (the PowerVR driver's second
      type on air/OS): with no coherent cached type, the staging heap
      (ZINK_HEAP_HOST_VISIBLE_COHERENT_CACHED) takes the cached types
      first, the coherent ones after them (Haiku only); coherent and
      persistent resources skip types that are not coherent (Haiku only);
      a read map of such a buffer needs no second staging copy (Haiku
      only); and zink_image_map() invalidates a non-coherent staging buffer
      or linear image before it is read, which it did not (any OS). Every
      GL frame's readback (glReadPixels, the BGLView present) read from
      write-combined memory before
  src/c11/impl/threads_posix.c
      thrd_create() gives Mesa's threads (util_queue, u_thread_create(),
      the Vulkan runtime's submit thread) 8 MiB of stack, as on Linux,
      instead of Haiku's 256 KiB: zink's flush thread overflowed it in the
      PowerVR driver's transfer submission (the pvr patch also moves that
      frame to the heap); and each of them touches its thread-local storage
      twice before it runs anything. Haiku's runtime loader starts a new
      thread's TLS vector at generation 0, so after any library with TLS
      was unloaded, the thread's second TLS access dropped what the first
      wrote. Summit's zink cache thread called util_call_once_data()'s lost
      callback, NULL. The runtime loader needs the real fix
      (tls_generation_check tests it); this keeps Mesa's threads safe
      without it
  src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_state.c, zink_descriptors.c,
  src/compiler/nir/nir_opt_varyings.c
      the three largest stack frames left (zink_create_vertex_state() 109
      KiB: a whole zink_context; zink_descriptor_program_init() 85 KiB;
      backward_inter_shader_code_motion() 70 KiB) on the heap instead: they
      run on the application's GL thread, 256 KiB by default on Haiku
  src/mesa/state_tracker/st_cb_flush.c
      a polled device reset is not also replayed as a callback event
  src/gallium/auxiliary/draw/draw_decompose_tmp.h, draw_vs_exec.c
      softpipe: quad-strip edge flags after rotation; the TGSI machine's
      cached token address is dropped when its shader is deleted
  include/renderdoc_app.h
      calling convention on Haiku

Not ported: V3D/Mali screens, texture caches, GPU readback and shared
bitmaps (driver-specific); the x86_64 fork's pipeline-library checks
(66c695e: the PowerVR driver has no graphics pipeline libraries), Summit's
parallel readback copies (e6152fd) and NVIDIA scan-out (f4e58bd).

